REPRODUCED (critic/tail_check.py, tail_check.json, using a separate day-clustered IRLS):
- pooled dev OOS slope 0.984 (s.e. 0.188) on 48,851 stock-days over 378 days;
- folds 1.307 (0.635), 1.353 (0.300), 0.652 (0.157);
- within-day slope 0.499 (0.071); between-day 1.426 (0.321);
- 2026: slope 0.801 (0.118), within 0.731 (0.120), between 0.952 (0.362);
- Cochran Q 4.90 on 2 df; precision-weighted dev slope 0.826 (0.136).
The diagnosis therefore holds up numerically. The 2026 slope sits inside the dev fold range. The within-day over-dispersion is the one robust finding (below 1 in every period; dev z about -7).

No candidate meets the declared rule. The best Brier gain has t = 0.66 (C3), so any SPA family p-value is at least the single-candidate one-sided p, about 0.25. C1's frozen recalibration would have raised the level and hurt 2026, so rejecting it is right.

